Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 19:34:17 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: ECON: Futures vs. Equities (was: Stupidity and natural selection)

> From: pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)
> 
>  pmetzger@shearson.com  (Perry E. Metzger) writes:
> >never really went anywhere. Again, the futures contract (or options
> >contract) is a zero sum game but the actual equity is not. Remember, one
> >is a bet, and the other is a purchase of a tangible. There is a big difference
> >globally if what I have done is bought a house versus betting someone
> >on what the value of the house will be in six months. One is the aquisition
> >of a tangible asset -- no one need lose money on that. On the other hand,
> >someone always loses money in a bet, by definition.
> 
>  Nonsense. It is perfectly possible for a futures or options contract
> to be a purchase of the underlying tangible. These contracts are
> obligations to deliver something on a specified date, and if they
> are not repurchased by the original seller, that delivery will happen.
> It is true that almost all such contracts are created by short sales
> and are liquidated without any delivery of tangibles (in which case
> they amount to bets), but there are still a few transactions which
> involve real assets.

I will readily admit that such contracts can be written with the party
writing the option or future already being in possession of the underlying
good. However, there is no requirement that this be the case, it generally
is not the case, and with the advent of cash settlement in many markets
it is increasingly ceasing to be the case at all. Virtually all the financials
cash settle, for example.

Perry

