Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 17:36:30 PST
From: raphael@fx.com (Glen Raphael)
Subject: Cholesterol levels and meat eating

>> Bzzt.  Recent studies show that in somewhere between 2/3 and 3/4 of the
>> population, intake of animal fat has *absolutely no* effect on serum
>> cholesterol and lipoprotein levels. This hasn't made it to the diet books yet
>
>Well, then I guess that Dean Ornish's study was all junk then, and the
>epidemiological studies comparing world populations were also all junk.
>There have been repeated studies on this. Animal fat per se may be meaningless,
>but meat is high in fat almost no matter what you do compared to, say,
>rice or spinach. Cholesterol intake per se might be meaningless, but fat
>intake and the amount of cholesterol you yourself produce are correlated.

For the population as a whole, yes. But for many individual members of it,
no.  I'm sorry, Perry, but your information is out of date.

My best friend Char and his fiance Cara (both med students) were part of a UCSD
medical school cholesterol study two years ago. Both went on a pure liquid
diet (plus some vegetables) for 6 months. No milk, no oils, no meat, and
they got their cholesterol levels checked twice a week. The result? Cara's
cholesterol level slowly declined, ending significantly below what it had
been. But Char's cholesterol level stayed *exactly the same*.  Char is
"cholesterol-insensitive"; his body produces more to compensate if he eats
less meat. As far as reducing cholesterol is concerned, there is no benefit to
char from going vegan. This is apparently a not-uncommon phenomenon.

Glen Raphael
raphael@fx.com

